# Rust FFI and Unsafe Boundaries

## Scope

This file owns Rust-side `unsafe`, ABI, and FFI boundary rules. Keep general
Rust style in `rust_style.mdc` and cross-language error policy in
`error_handling.mdc`.

## Unsafe Code

- Prefer safe Rust by default; isolate `unsafe` into the smallest practical
  block or helper.
- Every `unsafe` block must have a nearby `SAFETY:` comment explaining the
  invariants that make it sound.
- Every `unsafe fn` must document its caller obligations in a `# Safety`
  section.
- Do not hide unsoundness behind a safe API. A safe wrapper must validate all
  invariants required by the unsafe operation it contains.
- Avoid widening lifetimes or aliasing guarantees beyond what the foreign API or
  owner can actually provide.

## FFI Entry Points

- Validate pointers, lengths, alignment, nullability, and ownership before
  constructing references, slices, or strings.
- Treat all data from Dart, C ABI callers, ODBC drivers, environment variables,
  and dynamically loaded libraries as untrusted.
- Ensure panics never cross non-unwinding FFI boundaries. Catch and translate
  boundary failures to explicit error codes or stored structured errors.
- Keep FFI entry points thin: validate inputs, call safe internal code, convert
  outputs, and return ABI-safe status.
- Do not store foreign pointers beyond the documented lifetime unless ownership
  has been transferred and release is defined.

## ABI and Layout

- Use `#[repr(C)]` for FFI-facing structs and enums exposed across the boundary.
- Use ABI-stable primitive types, pointers, and explicit lengths at the boundary.
- Do not expose Rust-specific layout types such as `String`, `Vec<T>`,
  `Result<T, E>`, trait objects, or non-`repr(C)` enums across FFI.
- Version wire formats and ABI changes deliberately; preserve compatibility
  unless the project explicitly accepts a breaking native contract change.
- Keep cbindgen headers, `.def` exports, Dart FFI bindings, and native symbols
  aligned when exported functions change.

## ABI Change Checklist

- Update the Rust export, symbol list, cbindgen output, and Dart FFI binding
  together.
- Preserve old exported functions when compatibility requires delegation instead
  of replacement.
- Version new wire formats and keep decoders backward-compatible when older
  payloads are still supported.
- Add or update ABI, symbol, layout, wire-format, and Dart/native alignment
  tests for every exported contract change.
- Document intentional breaking ABI changes in the changelog or native docs.

## Allocation and Resources

- Make allocation and release responsibilities explicit for every buffer,
  handle, string, and native resource.
- Free memory with the same allocator and layer that allocated it.
- Prefer RAII internally and expose explicit release functions only at the ABI
  edge.
- Make handle ownership, invalidation, and thread-safety rules explicit.
- On partial failure, release already-acquired native resources before returning.

## Concurrency

- Treat shared handles, ODBC connections, global registries, and telemetry state
  as concurrency-sensitive.
- Require explicit synchronization for mutable shared state.
- Do not assume a foreign caller serializes calls unless the ABI contract says
  so and tests cover it.
